Fixed, with one deliberate deviation from the suggested diff: the throw is deferred to `afterAll` rather than raised in `afterEach`.

Failed entries are now retained instead of discarded:

```ts
} catch (error) {
  failedKeys.push({ provider, keyName });
  // ... existing logging
}
createdKeys.splice(0, createdKeys.length, ...failedKeys);
```

and a new `afterAll` fails the run with the leaked key names if anything survives.

The reason for not throwing in `afterEach`: this describe sets `mode: "serial"`, and per the Playwright docs "If one of the tests fails, all subsequent tests are skipped." Throwing in `afterEach` would therefore skip every remaining test in the file, so their `afterEach` hooks would never run — which is exactly the retry the retained entries exist for. Deferring to `afterAll` keeps the loud failure while letting the intervening hooks actually retry.

Also worth recording: no manual reset of `createdKeys` is needed before the throw, because "Should any test fail, Playwright Test will discard the entire worker process along with the browser and will start a new one" — a retry re-imports the module with fresh state.

Verified: `tsc --noEmit` clean for this file, and all 5 tests still collect via `playwright test --list`.

Note the same discard-on-cleanup-failure pattern exists in `providers.spec.ts` (lines 18-48, both the `createdKeys` and `createdProviders` loops) — this spec was copy-pasted from it. Left out of this PR to keep it scoped to the UI change, but it is the same leak and worth a follow-up.

